    "seo": {
      "title": "History of Public Education in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "How local school initiatives became a national education system, and why access, curriculum, and equality developed unevenly."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "beginnings",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From educational reform to public schooling",
        "content": "Iran's modern public-school system emerged gradually from initiatives with different purposes and constituencies. Dar al-Funun, the polytechnic established in Tehran in 1851 by the reforming minister Amir Kabir, initially trained elite students for technical, military, and medical work. Its importance should not be confused with universal elementary provision: training officials and educating an entire population were different projects. [s1]\n\nElementary-school pioneers widened that ambition. Hasan Roshdiyeh established a modern primary school in Tabriz in 1888 and subsequently worked in Tehran. The Anjoman-e Maaref, an educational association formed in 1897, helped establish and supervise schools. Early institutions lacked a common curriculum and standardized teaching materials; their founders exercised considerable authority. Schools serving religious minorities and other local constituencies were also part of this varied educational landscape. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "legislation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An entitlement before universal delivery",
        "content": "The Fundamental Law of Education, enacted in 1911 by the Majles, Iran's parliament, required parents or guardians to arrange education for children from age seven and made instruction in public primary schools free. Yet the law itself distinguished village schools from urban schools. Smaller villages could have shorter courses and teachers with fewer educational qualifications. Public responsibility therefore developed within an unequal geography of provision. A legal requirement did not establish that every child could attend an adequately staffed school. [s2]\n\nDuring the 1920s and 1930s, state centralization brought greater administrative integration and expanding enrollment. Nevertheless, the resulting system remained disproportionately urban and socially selective. Later expansion, especially after the Second World War, extended elementary schooling into rural areas. Ahmad Ashraf's historical survey emphasizes that access and literacy improved while differences between regions, women and men, and rural and urban populations persisted. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "revolution",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Revolution, reconstruction, and competing objectives",
        "content": "The 1979 revolution changed education's declared political and cultural purposes. In her study of 1979–95, education scholar Golnar Mehran describes textbook revision and an explicit effort to form citizens committed to Islamic and revolutionary values. Her account also documents expanded educational opportunities, particularly outside cities. Ideological change and broader participation occurred together; one cannot be used as a substitute measure for the other. [s3]\n\nMehran distinguishes the revolutionary and wartime period from the reconstruction years following the 1988 cease-fire with Iraq. During reconstruction, official priorities placed greater emphasis on skilled workers, educational quality, and reform of secondary schooling. These are documented historical priorities, rather than proof that the intended improvements were achieved everywhere. [s3]\n\nThis history is best read through separate questions: who entered school, what they learned, who controlled instruction, and what opportunities followed. Those questions prevent school expansion from becoming a simple success narrative and prevent political changes from obscuring families' growing participation in education. The evidence summarized here establishes the main historical transitions through the mid-1990s; it does not establish conditions in schools at the research cutoff. [s1][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
