    "updated_at": "2026-10-26T15:29:03Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Internal Migration through Iranian History | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iran's changing migration patterns, the rise of movement between cities, and the limits of census comparisons."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "definitions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Movement within the country",
        "content": "Internal migration concerns changes of residence within Iran. It must be distinguished from immigration into the country and emigration abroad. It also differs from urbanization: an increase in the population classified as urban does not, by itself, reveal how many people moved. The historical demographic literature identifies changes in the definition of a city and the incorporation of villages into cities as additional reasons urban population totals can increase. [s1]\n\nMeasurement matters just as much as geography. Comparing birthplace with residence identifies a different population from asking whether someone moved during a specified interval. Iran's census history illustrates the problem: the 1996 questionnaire omitted birthplace, interrupting the ability to quantify lifetime migrants using that question. Such a break should not be interpreted as evidence that migration itself diminished. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "changing-pattern",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Beyond the village-to-city narrative",
        "content": "Migration is often imagined as a single journey from a village to a large city. That pathway matters, but it does not describe the whole system. A historical table reproduced in a KNOMAD report shows an increasing urban-to-urban share across successive reporting intervals. The chart below preserves those intervals rather than presenting the observations as annual measurements. [s2]\n\nThe composition of migration and its volume answer different questions. A larger share of moves between urban places does not establish a larger total number of migrants, greater travel distances, or movement exclusively toward Tehran. Nor does this table identify how often an individual moved, whether a move was temporary, or whether a household subsequently returned. [s2]\n\nThe report's account of the 2011–2016 period also identifies contrasting provincial experiences: Semnan, Alborz, and Yazd had high positive migration rates, while Lorestan, Chaharmahal-Bakhtiari, and Ilam had high negative rates. This regional variation is more informative than assuming a uniform national experience. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "urban-migration-share",
        "type": "chart",
        "content": {
          "type": "bar",
          "title": "Urban-to-urban share of internal migration in Iran",
          "dataset_id": "migration-composition",
          "x_field": "period_label",
          "series_ids": ["urban-urban-share"],
          "x_axis_label": "Source reporting interval; intervals have unequal lengths",
          "y_axis_label": "Share of internal migrants (%)",
          "y_axis_min": 0,
          "show_legend": false,
          "connect_nulls": false,
          "interpretation": "The reproduced table reports an urban-to-urban share of 40% in 1976–1986 and 68% in 2011–2016. These are interval shares, not annual migration rates or counts. [s2]"
        },
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "motives-and-limits",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why people move, and what remains uncertain",
        "content": "UNFPA's publication introduction describes several motives, including employment, leaving deprivation, retirement, and seeking a different residential environment. Its examples include movement away from cities as well as toward them. This framing helps explain why a single rural-push or urban-pull story is insufficient, although the introduction alone cannot establish the relative importance of each motive. [s3]\n\nEnvironmental conditions also deserve careful treatment. A CESifo working paper using provincial census evidence from 2011 and 2016 reports a positive relationship between aerosol optical depth, a satellite-based pollution measure, and net outmigration. That statistical finding concerns aggregate provincial patterns; it does not identify the reason for every household's move. [s4]\n\nRead together, these sources support a history of changing destinations and multiple motivations. They also show why contemporary claims require fresh evidence: retrospective census intervals and a later targeted survey cannot supply a continuous national annual migration series. Earlier historical mobility and post-2016 national patterns remain research gaps in this account. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
