    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-1-8",
    "page_path": "/explore/history/history-of-science-and-technology/",
    "name": "History of Science & Technology",
    "level": 2,
    "parent_path": "/explore/history/",
    "entity_type": "category",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran's scientific and technological history includes mathematical research, medical writing, hydraulic engineering, and the reproduction of knowledge in manuscripts and print. These developments belonged to multilingual networks extending beyond modern national borders, and their surviving evidence varies considerably.",
    "tags": ["History of science", "Technology", "Astronomy", "Medicine", "Engineering", "Printing"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-26T15:29:03Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-26T15:29:03Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "History of Science & Technology in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore scientific learning, medicine, astronomy, qanat engineering, and printing through documented objects, institutions, and debates."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Knowledge across languages and borders",
        "content": "A useful history of science in Iran follows people, texts, instruments, and working practices. The Library of Congress's Persian-book exhibition shows why language and geography must be distinguished: scholars of Persian origin often wrote scientific works in Arabic, while Persian scientific writing circulated beyond Iran, including at the Mughal court in India. A Persian-language manuscript is therefore evidence of a linguistic network, not automatically an object produced within today's Iranian borders. [s1]\n\nThe exhibition also places cosmography beside anatomy, pharmacology, and geography. These materials should be read in their historical categories. A cosmography describing the world's wonders could include mythical creatures; its presence in a scientific collection does not make every description an observation. The surviving book establishes what was written, illustrated, translated, and collected. Assessing the accuracy and practical use of its contents requires further evidence. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "learning-and-medicine",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Centers of learning: evidence before superlatives",
        "content": "Gondeshapur, also spelled Jondeshapur, in southwestern Iran is frequently presented as a great ancient medical university. The institutional scale implied by that description is disputed. Encyclopaedia Iranica's discussion of Greek medicine challenges claims for a great teaching hospital and a uniquely dominant channel carrying Greek medicine to Baghdad. It instead situates the city among several centers of Syriac scholarship where medicine was taught and practiced. [s2]\n\nThis distinction matters for the history of medicine and for the branch on ancient learning. The existence of scholarly and medical activity does not establish a modern university structure, a precise founding date, or a claim to be the world's first university. The responsible question is which activities and relationships particular sources document. An overview can acknowledge the city's importance while leaving disputed institutional reconstructions open. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "astronomy",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Astronomy as collaborative research",
        "content": "Maragha, in northwestern Iran, offers a more closely documented institutional example. Construction of its observatory began in 1259 under the patronage of the Mongol ruler Hulegu. Historian George Saliba places its operation around 1261–62 and describes Nasir al-Din Tusi's collaboration with astronomers and instrument specialists, including Muayyad al-Din al-Urdi from Damascus. [s3]\n\nThe work involved more than recording positions in the sky. Researchers examined inherited planetary models and developed mathematical alternatives to particular features of Greek astronomy. Tusi also reworked earlier mathematical texts, comparing translations and revising explanations. This makes the observatory a useful meeting point for astronomy, mathematics, instruments, and patronage. Its achievements arose from a research community, rather than from an isolated genius working outside political and material circumstances. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "engineering",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Engineering below the surface",
        "content": "A qanat is a gently sloping underground water channel that draws from an aquifer and carries water by gravity toward settlements and fields. UNESCO's description explains how access shafts support excavation and ventilation, while the tunnel's gradient requires specialized knowledge. Engineering here includes construction, maintenance, and the organization of water distribution. [s4]\n\nThe Persian Qanat World Heritage property represents selected systems, rather than every qanat in Iran. Its documentation connects hydraulic structures with reservoirs, mills, gardens, and settlement patterns. This is a useful entry into architectural history because buildings and landscapes depended on supporting infrastructure. UNESCO's account describes communal management traditions; it should not be treated as independent proof that every historical or contemporary distribution arrangement was equitable. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "qanat-process",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "How a qanat carries water",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Aquifer\"] -->|\"Supplies groundwater\"| B[\"Collection tunnel\"]\n  C[\"Access shafts\"] -->|\"Enable excavation and ventilation\"| B\n  B -->|\"Gentle slope permits gravity flow\"| D[\"Outlet\"]\n  D -->|\"Feeds\"| E[\"Distribution channels\"]\n  E -->|\"Carry water to\"| F[\"Agricultural land\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows distinguish water movement from the construction and ventilation role of access shafts. This is a simplified physical process, not a map of a particular qanat or a model of water ownership. [s4]",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "printing",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Printing changed how knowledge traveled",
        "content": "Nineteenth-century lithography reproduced writing from prepared stone surfaces and helped connect established calligraphic skills with mechanical book production. In Iran, booksellers, copyists, and printers participated in a growing trade centered especially on Tehran and Tabriz. The process did not simply erase manuscript culture: copied books and lithographed books continued alongside one another. [s5]\n\nDar al-Funun, Tehran's modern polytechnic, established a lithographic press after opening in 1851. Its teaching publications included scientific manuals, linking printing with technical education. This branch therefore connects naturally to the histories of medicine and communication: knowledge depended on reproducible teaching materials as well as discoveries. [s5]\n\nTaken together, manuscripts, observatories, water systems, and printing houses suggest complementary ways to explore this history. Each asks who possessed knowledge, how it was tested or applied, and how it reached others. None alone supplies a continuous national measure of scientific achievement. [s1][s3][s4][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s4", "s5"]
      }
    ],
